  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186147454,
      "id": "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da",
      "title": "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "128dcdde-aa59-5064-8935-1c3c2bebbd46",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{(x-1)}^{2}y={(x-1)}^{2}{y}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x-1>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which all displayed real expressions are defined.\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-3{(x-1)}^{2},\\,q(x)={(x-1)}^{2},\\,n=-4.\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the differential equation by ${y}^{4},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{4}\\frac{dy}{dx}-3{(x-1)}^{2}{y}^{5}={(x-1)}^{2}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{5}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}^{4}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-3{(x-1)}^{2}z={(x-1)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{(x-1)}^{2}z=5{(x-1)}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-15{(x-1)}^{2},\\,Q(x)=5{(x-1)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-15{(x-1)}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{(x-1)}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}\\cdot (5{(x-1)}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-15{(x-1)}^{2}z{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}} & =5{(x-1)}^{2}{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}) & =5{(x-1)}^{2}{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{(x-1)}^{2}{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}} & =-\\frac{{e}^{-5{(x-1)}^{3}}}{3}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{5{(x-1)}^{3}}-1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{5}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{5}=\\frac{C{e}^{5{(x-1)}^{3}}-1}{3}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\sqrt[5]{\\frac{C{e}^{5{(x-1)}^{3}}-1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are the nonzero real solutions on the specified intervals; exclude denominator zeros and restrict constants and intervals to the real branches used above.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "3c0c301c-e52c-5b11-b0ea-0b690303f003",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "01275566-f555-55e4-94e7-3fe77c9f9f5f",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{C{e}^{3{(x-1)}^{2}}-1}{2}}"
            },
