  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186147454,
      "id": "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da",
      "title": "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1f7773da-735d-521e-af31-a36f311ce4fd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-(x+3){y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x+3>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which all displayed real expressions are defined.\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=1,\\,q(x)=-(x+3),\\,n=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{3},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}\\frac{dy}{dx}+{y}^{-2}=-(x+3).\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-(x+3)\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z=2\\cdot (x+3)\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-2,\\,Q(x)=2\\cdot (x+3)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (-2)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z) & ={e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}\\cdot (2\\cdot (x+3)) \\\\ {e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2z{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)} & =2\\cdot (x+3){e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}) & =2\\cdot (x+3){e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\cdot (x+3){e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2\\cdot (x+3)}(2\\cdot (x+3)+1)+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C{e}^{2\\cdot (x+3)}-2\\cdot (x+3)-1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-2}=\\frac{C{e}^{2\\cdot (x+3)}-2\\cdot (x+3)-1}{2}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2\\cdot (x+3)}-2\\cdot (x+3)-1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are the nonzero real solutions on the specified intervals; exclude denominator zeros and restrict constants and intervals to the real branches used above.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "ef67be8d-d641-561c-a20a-21d8aa05a24b",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "7b544564-0402-564d-a20b-c261b5772916",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\frac{2}{C{e}^{(x+3)}-2\\cdot (x+3)}"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "59c2e675-e81d-537b-84f4-232a823318ac",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{C{e}^{2\\cdot (x+3)}-2\\cdot (x+3)-1}}"
