  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186147454,
      "id": "17061f4e-da7c-58f9-90b4-47bdc21505da",
      "title": "Solving a Bernoulli Differential Equation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "72d5d166-b700-5555-a7ae-2ad753d02dcb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{(x+4)}=\\sqrt{(x+4)}\\,{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $x+4>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which all displayed real expressions are defined.\n\n{{selection}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-\\frac{1}{(x+4)},\\,q(x)=\\sqrt{(x+4)},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-\\frac{1}{(x+4)}{y}^{-1}=\\sqrt{(x+4)}.\\,\\,(\u2217)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\u27f9\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{(x+4)}z=\\sqrt{(x+4)}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)}z=-\\sqrt{(x+4)}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)},\\,Q(x)=-\\sqrt{(x+4)}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/(x+4))\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+4)} \\\\  & =(x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=(x+4)$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)}z) & =(x+4)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{(x+4)}) \\\\ (x+4)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =-(x+4)\\sqrt{(x+4)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+4)z) & =-{(x+4)}^{3/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}((x+4)z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{(x+4)}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ (x+4)z & =-\\frac{2{(x+4)}^{5/2}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-2{(x+4)}^{5/2}}{5\\cdot (x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-2{(x+4)}^{5/2}}{5\\cdot (x+4)}\\,\u21d2\\,y=\\frac{5\\cdot (x+4)}{C-2{(x+4)}^{5/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese are the nonzero real solutions on the specified intervals; exclude denominator zeros and restrict constants and intervals to the real branches used above.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8cb0f8eb-4e10-5456-879a-6a896e84d8e1",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "eb61a5d0-1fa6-5010-a67f-f86488250a4e",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\frac{5\\cdot (x+4)}{C+{(x+4)}^{3/2}}"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "5808ced8-670c-54d3-b5fc-81b839c766b2",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\frac{{(x+4)}^{2}}{C-5{(x+4)}^{5/2}}"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "abda7b7b-d211-51ad-a1bc-9e9e2a901da8",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y=\\frac{5\\cdot (x+4)}{C-2{(x+4)}^{5/2}}"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "11487e03-f873-54c9-9f10-55766aba2e30",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "y={(\\frac{2\\cdot (x+4)}{C-{(x+4)}^{3/2}})}^{2}"
